# TypeScript

- Enable `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, and `noImplicitOverride`.
- Do not use `any`, `@ts-ignore`, or unchecked type assertions in application code.
- Accept `unknown` at untrusted boundaries and narrow it with schemas or type guards.
- Validate environment variables, request input, CMS configuration, and external responses at runtime.
- Model domain states with discriminated unions instead of overlapping booleans.
- Prefer `satisfies` when checking object shapes without widening useful literal types.
- Use explicit return types on exported functions and shared hooks; infer local implementation details.
- Keep types close to their owning feature; export only contracts used across module boundaries.
- Use branded or validated values for identifiers and slugs when confusing plain strings is risky.
- Never swallow an error. Add context, preserve the cause, and return a typed user-safe result when expected.
- Avoid non-null assertions. Prove the invariant or handle the missing case.
- Exhaustively handle unions with a `never` check when a missing branch could change behavior.
